<!--
  Wazuh custom rules — MSc dissertation detection lab
  Canonical copy. Deploys to /var/ossec/etc/rules/local_rules.xml on Blue (10.10.10.10).

  Rule IDs are allocated in RULE_ID_REGISTER.md. Never invent an ID — Wazuh uses the FIRST
  occurrence of a duplicate and silently ignores the rest.

  After deploying:
    sudo /var/ossec/bin/wazuh-analysisd -t      # must be clean
    sudo systemctl restart wazuh-manager

  Chain child rules from sysmon_eid1_detections — the group Sysmon EID 1 events land in AFTER
  matching a built-in detection rule. NOT sysmon_event1. Verify against the `groups` field of a
  real event in Discover.
-->

<group name="attack,discovery,local,">

  <!--
    T1087.001 Local Account Discovery via net.exe
    Custom rule ID 100200 | level 10

    Default Wazuh rule 92031 detects this at level 3 and maps it to the PARENT technique T1087.
    This rule adds sub-technique precision (T1087.001) at proportionate severity. That contrast is
    the dissertation's core argument in miniature.

    Two conditions, ANDed:
      1. originalFileName is net.exe or net1.exe — harder to evade than matching the image path,
         since renaming the binary does not change the PE version resource.
      2. commandLine contains an account/group enumeration subcommand.

    Condition 2 was added after review. Without it the rule fired on every net.exe invocation
    (net use, net start, net view, net share) and mapped all of them to T1087.001 — imprecise in
    exactly the dimension the thesis claims to improve, and it would have inflated the positive
    class in the ML dataset with routine drive mappings.
  -->
  <!--
    ⚠️ PRECISION FIX 2026-08-08, found while preparing T1136.001 Create Local Account.

    As written, this rule matched `net user <name> /add` because that command line contains "user" - so
    ACCOUNT CREATION would have been reported as ACCOUNT DISCOVERY. That is the same class of error this
    project measures in the default ruleset: right family, wrong technique. `net user X /add` is
    T1136.001, `net user X /delete` is T1531 Account Access Removal, and neither is discovery.

    The negated condition below excludes the modifying switches, leaving 100200 to match enumeration
    only. Verified safe for the existing T1087.001 result: its atomics run `net user`, `net localgroup`
    and `quser` with no /add, /delete or /active, so the recorded measurements are unchanged.

    Caught by asking "which existing rule will fire on the NEXT technique's commands?" before running it -
    the same cross-rule check that was added to the register after T1016.
  -->
  <rule id="100200" level="10">
    <if_group>sysmon_eid1_detections</if_group>
    <field name="win.eventdata.originalFileName" type="pcre2">(?i)net1?\.exe</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.commandLine" type="pcre2">(?i)\s+(user|localgroup|group|accounts)\b</field>
    <!-- /del is a valid abbreviation of /delete and net.exe accepts it. The first version of this
         negation matched only the long form, so `net user /del <name>` still fired 100200 and account
         DELETION was reported as account DISCOVERY. Observed twice in the T1136.001 baseline. Windows
         command-line switches are routinely abbreviated - match the short forms too. -->
    <field name="win.eventdata.commandLine" type="pcre2" negate="yes">(?i)\s/(add|delete|del|active|act)\b</field>
    <description>T1087.001 Local Account Discovery via net.exe</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1087.001</id>
    </mitre>
  </rule>

  <!--
    ===================================================================================================
    T1082 System Information Discovery
    Custom rule IDs 100230-100233 | level 8

    MEASURED BASELINE (2026-08-06, 5 detonation windows, 14 atomic tests, 146 alerts):
    stock Wazuh detected T1082 ZERO times. What fired instead was generic execution telemetry:
        92052  level 4  cmd.exe by abnormal parent            -> T1059.003
        92032  level 3  Suspicious Windows cmd shell exec     -> T1087 + T1059.003
        92027  level 4  Powershell spawned powershell         -> T1059.001
    So the default ruleset does not merely lose precision on T1082 - it MISATTRIBUTES the activity to
    T1087 Account Discovery and T1059.003 Command Shell, i.e. the wrong technique AND the wrong tactic
    (Execution rather than Discovery). An analyst following those alerts investigates the wrong
    behaviour. That is a stronger finding than T1087.001's parent-only mapping, and these rules are the
    corrective.

    LEVEL RATIONALE: 8, against 10 for T1087.001. Both are Discovery, but host enumeration
    (systeminfo, hostname, OS version) is far more common in routine administration than account
    enumeration, so a lower level is proportionate. Severity is a deliberate, defensible choice here,
    not a copy of the previous rule.

    DESIGN, following the lesson recorded in RULE_ID_REGISTER.md note 1: match on originalFileName
    (survives binary renaming, since the PE version resource is unchanged) AND constrain the command
    line wherever the tool is multi-purpose. wmic and reg.exe do many things that are not discovery;
    systeminfo and hostname do exactly one thing each and need no command-line condition.

    ⚠️ VERIFY BEFORE TRUSTING: originalFileName values are asserted from the PE version resources, not
    yet observed in this lab's telemetry. Detonate T1082 and confirm each rule fires. If 100230 does
    not fire, check the actual originalFileName in Discover and adjust - a rule that silently never
    matches is indistinguishable from a technique that is not detected, which has already cost this
    project a day.
    ===================================================================================================
  -->

  <rule id="100230" level="8">
    <if_group>sysmon_eid1_detections</if_group>
    <field name="win.eventdata.originalFileName" type="pcre2">(?i)^(systeminfo|hostname)\.exe$</field>
    <description>T1082 System Information Discovery via native host-information utility</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1082</id>
    </mitre>
  </rule>

  <rule id="100231" level="8">
    <if_group>sysmon_eid1_detections</if_group>
    <field name="win.eventdata.originalFileName" type="pcre2">(?i)^wmic\.exe$</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.commandLine" type="pcre2">(?i)\b(os|bios|computersystem|csproduct|diskdrive|qfe|environment)\b</field>
    <description>T1082 System Information Discovery via WMIC host query</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1082</id>
    </mitre>
  </rule>

  <rule id="100232" level="8">
    <if_group>sysmon_eid1_detections</if_group>
    <field name="win.eventdata.originalFileName" type="pcre2">(?i)^reg\.exe$</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.commandLine" type="pcre2">(?i)(MachineGuid|CurrentBuildNumber|ProductName|SystemBiosVersion|VideoBiosVersion|International)</field>
    <description>T1082 System Information Discovery via registry query for host identity</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1082</id>
    </mitre>
  </rule>

  <!--
    Closes the cmdlet blind spot found in T1087.001: rule 100200 keys on net.exe and detected nothing
    from atomic T1087.001-9's Get-LocalUser / Get-LocalGroup, so a purely PowerShell adversary evaded
    it entirely. The same gap applies to T1082 tests 37 and 38 (Get-WinSystemLocale, Get-PSDrive).

    LIMITATION, and it is a real one: this only sees cmdlets passed on the command line via -Command
    or -File. It cannot see cmdlets typed into an interactive session, because no new process is
    created and EID 1 never fires. Full coverage requires PowerShell Script Block Logging (EID 4104) -
    which this endpoint's own SCA scan reports as NOT enabled ("Ensure 'Turn on PowerShell Script Block
    Logging' is set to 'Enabled'", CIS Win10 v4.0.0). Enabling it is a concrete, evidence-backed
    recommendation for the dissertation rather than an assumption.
  -->
  <rule id="100233" level="8">
    <if_group>sysmon_eid1_detections</if_group>
    <field name="win.eventdata.originalFileName" type="pcre2">(?i)^powershell(\.exe)?$</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.commandLine" type="pcre2">(?i)(Get-ComputerInfo|Get-WinSystemLocale|Get-PSDrive|Win32_OperatingSystem|Win32_ComputerSystem|Win32_BIOS|Win32_Processor)</field>
    <description>T1082 System Information Discovery via PowerShell cmdlet or WMI class</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1082</id>
    </mitre>
  </rule>

  <!--
    ===================================================================================================
    T1033 System Owner/User Discovery
    Custom rule IDs 100240-100241 | level 8

    MEASURED BASELINE (2026-08-07, 5 windows, atomic tests 1/4/5/6/7, 165 attack alerts):
    the default ruleset detects T1033 only PARTIALLY, and this is a third distinct failure mode -
    different from T1087.001's parent-only mapping and T1082's outright misattribution.

      * `qwinsta.exe` session enumeration IS correctly detected: default rule 92022
        ("Gathered user information from Remote Desktop Service sessions") maps to T1033. 19 alerts.
      * `whoami.exe` - the single most common way anyone establishes identity on a host - produces 15
        alerts, ALL of them rule 92032, which maps to **T1087 + T1059.003**. So the most common T1033
        behaviour is attributed to Account Discovery and Command Shell instead.
      * The benign class fired NO T1033-mapped rule at all.

    So the gap is not the technique as a whole; it is the dominant variant within the technique.

    DELIBERATELY NOT WRITTEN, and each omission is itself a finding:
      * No rule for `qwinsta` / `quser` session enumeration - default rule 92022 already maps it to
        T1033 correctly. Adding one would duplicate correct behaviour and inflate the apparent
        contribution of the custom ruleset. Where the default is right, say so.
      * No rule for `wmic useraccount get /ALL` (present in atomic T1033-1). Enumerating local user
        accounts is arguably **T1087.001**, not T1033. ART places it under T1033, so the atomic spans
        two techniques - recorded as a limitation rather than papered over with a rule that would
        mislabel it either way.
    ===================================================================================================
  -->

  <rule id="100240" level="8">
    <if_group>sysmon_eid1_detections</if_group>
    <field name="win.eventdata.originalFileName" type="pcre2">(?i)^whoami\.exe$</field>
    <description>T1033 System Owner/User Discovery via whoami</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1033</id>
    </mitre>
  </rule>

  <!--
    Closes the cmdlet path, as 100233 does for T1082. Atomic T1033-4 reads %USERNAME% via environment
    variables and T1033-5 calls [Security.Principal.WindowsIdentity]::GetCurrent() - neither spawns a
    distinctively named process, so nothing in the default ruleset attributes them to T1033.

    Same limitation as 100233: this only sees cmdlets passed via -Command or -File. Interactive use
    creates no new process and EID 1 never fires. Script Block Logging (EID 4104) is required for full
    coverage and the endpoint's own SCA scan reports it disabled.
  -->
  <rule id="100241" level="8">
    <if_group>sysmon_eid1_detections</if_group>
    <field name="win.eventdata.originalFileName" type="pcre2">(?i)^powershell(\.exe)?$</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.commandLine" type="pcre2">(?i)(WindowsIdentity|env:USERNAME|env:USERDOMAIN|::UserName|Get-LocalUser)</field>
    <description>T1033 System Owner/User Discovery via PowerShell identity enumeration</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1033</id>
    </mitre>
  </rule>

  <!--
    ===================================================================================================
    T1016 System Network Configuration Discovery
    Custom rule IDs 100250-100252 | level 8

    MEASURED BASELINE (2026-08-07, 5 windows, atomic tests 1/2/9, 44 attack alerts):
    NOTHING in the default ruleset maps to T1016. What fires is:
        92032  L3  Suspicious Windows cmd shell execution   -> T1087 + T1059.003
        92052  L4  cmd prompt by abnormal parent            -> T1059.003
        92036  L?  binary started by a Windows cmd shell    -> T1059.003 + T1574.001
    Same failure mode as T1082 - the activity is visible but attributed to Execution rather than
    Discovery, and T1016 never appears. Four techniques measured so far, three distinct failure modes:
    parent-only (T1087.001), misattribution (T1082, T1016), partial coverage (T1033).

    TEST SET DELIBERATELY NARROWED. The first T1016 baseline used tests 1/2/4/7/9 and had to be
    discarded: tests 4 (TrickBot Style) and 7 (Qakbot Recon) are multi-technique recon SEQUENCES that
    also run net, whoami and nltest, so custom rules 100200 (T1087.001) and 100240 (T1033) fired
    correctly inside a window labelled T1016 and single-technique attribution became impossible.
    See LABELLING_SCHEME.md 3a. Tests 1, 2 and 9 exercise T1016 alone - confirmed empirically: the
    re-run shows zero 1002xx rules in the attack class.

    PRECISION NOTE ON netsh: `netsh advfirewall ... show` is discovery, but `netsh advfirewall set`
    DISABLES the firewall and is T1562.004 Defense Evasion. 100251 therefore requires an enumeration
    verb. A rule matching netsh broadly would map firewall tampering to Discovery - the same class of
    error this project is measuring in the defaults.
    ===================================================================================================
  -->

  <rule id="100250" level="8">
    <if_group>sysmon_eid1_detections</if_group>
    <field name="win.eventdata.originalFileName" type="pcre2">(?i)^(ipconfig|arp|route|nbtstat|netstat|nslookup)\.exe$</field>
    <description>T1016 System Network Configuration Discovery via native network utility</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1016</id>
    </mitre>
  </rule>

  <rule id="100251" level="8">
    <if_group>sysmon_eid1_detections</if_group>
    <field name="win.eventdata.originalFileName" type="pcre2">(?i)^netsh\.exe$</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.commandLine" type="pcre2">(?i)\bshow\b</field>
    <description>T1016 System Network Configuration Discovery via netsh enumeration</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1016</id>
    </mitre>
  </rule>

  <!--
    `net config` reports workstation and server network configuration - T1016, distinct from the account
    enumeration subcommands 100200 matches. The two conditions are disjoint by construction, so a single
    net.exe invocation cannot fire both: 100200 requires user|localgroup|group|accounts, this requires
    config. Verified on the T1016 re-run, where net.exe fired neither 100200 nor anything else.
  -->
  <rule id="100252" level="8">
    <if_group>sysmon_eid1_detections</if_group>
    <field name="win.eventdata.originalFileName" type="pcre2">(?i)^net1?\.exe$</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.commandLine" type="pcre2">(?i)\s+config\b</field>
    <description>T1016 System Network Configuration Discovery via net config</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1016</id>
    </mitre>
  </rule>

  <!--
    ===================================================================================================
    T1053.005 Scheduled Task
    Custom rule IDs 100270-100271 | level 10

    MEASURED BASELINE (2026-08-08, 4 usable windows, atomics 1/2/4/6/9, 48 attack alerts):
    NOTHING in the default ruleset maps to T1053.005. What fires is:
        92004  -> T1059.003          92027  -> T1059.001
        92032  -> T1087 + T1059.003  92052  -> T1059.003
    Every one is EXECUTION. A Persistence technique is read as Execution - a TACTIC-level miss, and the
    starkest of the seven techniques measured so far. Creating a scheduled task that survives reboot is
    materially different from running a command, and an analyst triaging these alerts would see only
    "powershell spawned powershell" with no indication that anything was left behind.

    LEVEL 10, not 8. Persistence outranks Discovery: a scheduled task grants an attacker return access
    after reboot, whereas host enumeration is reconnaissance. Severity here reflects consequence.

    TWO RULES, split by mechanism:
      100270  schtasks.exe /create or /change  - the native path
      100271  PowerShell and WMI task cmdlets  - Register/Set/New-ScheduledTask, MSFT_ScheduledTask

    PRECISION NOTES:
      * 100270 requires /create or /change. `schtasks /query` is ENUMERATION, not persistence, and the
        benign mirror uses it - matching it would map task listing to Persistence.
      * `schtasks /delete` is deliberately NOT matched. Deleting a task is T1070.009 Indicator Removal,
        a different technique, and it is also what this lab's own cleanup does between runs.
      * 100271 deliberately excludes `Get-ScheduledTask` for the same reason as /query.
    ===================================================================================================
  -->

  <rule id="100270" level="10">
    <if_group>sysmon_eid1_detections</if_group>
    <field name="win.eventdata.originalFileName" type="pcre2">(?i)^schtasks\.exe$</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.commandLine" type="pcre2">(?i)\s/(create|change)\b</field>
    <description>T1053.005 Scheduled Task created or modified via schtasks</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1053.005</id>
    </mitre>
  </rule>

  <rule id="100271" level="10">
    <if_group>sysmon_eid1_detections</if_group>
    <field name="win.eventdata.originalFileName" type="pcre2">(?i)^powershell(\.exe)?$</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.commandLine" type="pcre2">(?i)(Register-ScheduledTask|Set-ScheduledTask|New-ScheduledTask|MSFT_ScheduledTask)</field>
    <description>T1053.005 Scheduled Task created or modified via PowerShell or WMI</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1053.005</id>
    </mitre>
  </rule>

  <!--
    ===================================================================================================
    T1136.001 Create Local Account
    Custom rule IDs 100280-100283 | level 10 (100283 level 8)

    NOTE: no literal ampersand appears anywhere in this file. Wazuh parses rule files with its own
    OS_ReadXML, not a standards-compliant parser, and a bare ampersand has been known to fail the load
    even inside a comment. "ATTACK" below means MITRE ATT and CK.

    MEASURED BASELINE (2026-08-08, 5+5 windows, atomics 4/5/8/9, 156 attack alerts):
    a DIFFERENT failure mode from the previous seven - the default ruleset detects the behaviour
    accurately and describes it correctly, then tags it with the WRONG ATTACK technique:

        60109  "User account enabled or created"   -> T1098 Account Manipulation
        60110  "User account changed"              -> T1098
        60111  "User account disabled or deleted"  -> T1098 + T1531
        60154  "Administrators Group Changed"      -> T1484 Domain Policy Modification
        60160  "Domain Users Group Changed"        -> T1484
        60170  "Users Group Changed"               -> T1484

    Rule 60109 fires on Windows Security event 4720, whose literal meaning is "a user account was
    created" - that is T1136.001 by definition. Wazuh maps it to T1098 Account Manipulation, a SIBLING
    technique. And the group rules map local group membership changes to T1484 Domain Policy
    Modification, which is not only the wrong technique but describes a domain mechanism this
    WORKGROUP-only endpoint does not have.

    So this is SIBLING MISATTRIBUTION: same tactic family, adjacent technique, correct description.
    Distinct from T1087.001 (parent-only), T1082/T1016/T1053.005 (wrong tactic entirely) and T1033
    (partial coverage). Arguably the most insidious of the five, because the alert text reads correctly
    and only the ATTACK tag is wrong - so a coverage matrix built from these mappings would show
    T1136.001 as uncovered while showing T1098 and T1484 as covered, both incorrectly.

    APPROACH: 100280 REFINES rather than duplicates. It chains from 60109 via if_sid, so the default
    rule still does the detecting and the custom rule corrects the attribution. That is a different and
    cheaper remediation than writing detection from scratch, and worth contrasting in the write-up with
    T1082/T1016 where no default detection existed to refine.
    ===================================================================================================
  -->

  <rule id="100280" level="10">
    <if_sid>60109</if_sid>
    <description>T1136.001 Local account created (re-attributed from T1098)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1136.001</id>
    </mitre>
  </rule>

  <rule id="100281" level="10">
    <if_group>sysmon_eid1_detections</if_group>
    <field name="win.eventdata.originalFileName" type="pcre2">(?i)^net1?\.exe$</field>
    <!-- Argument order is NOT fixed: the atomic uses `net user /add "name" "pass"` while an
         administrator more often types `net user name pass /add`. Two drafts failed a dry-run against
         both forms before this one - the first required a token between `user` and `/add`, the second
         wrote `\s+user\s.*\s/add` which consumes the single space in `user /add` and then demands a
         second one. Terminate the keyword with `\b`, not `\s`, so the separator stays available.
         `localgroup` cannot match because it lacks the `user` keyword. -->
    <field name="win.eventdata.commandLine" type="pcre2">(?i)\s+user\b.*?\s/add\b</field>
    <description>T1136.001 Local account created via net user /add</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1136.001</id>
    </mitre>
  </rule>

  <rule id="100282" level="10">
    <if_group>sysmon_eid1_detections</if_group>
    <field name="win.eventdata.originalFileName" type="pcre2">(?i)^powershell(\.exe)?$</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.commandLine" type="pcre2">(?i)(New-LocalUser|AccountManagement\.UserPrincipal|ADSI.*\bCreate\b.*user)</field>
    <description>T1136.001 Local account created via PowerShell or .NET</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1136.001</id>
    </mitre>
  </rule>

  <!-- 100283: local group membership change. NOT T1136.001 - adding an existing principal to a group is
       T1098 Account Manipulation. Included here because the default rules 60154/60160/60170 tag it
       T1484 Domain Policy Modification, which is wrong on two counts: wrong technique, and a domain
       mechanism this WORKGROUP endpoint does not have. Kept at level 8 (below the creation rules) so
       the ordering of alert severity reflects the ordering of adversary progress. -->
  <rule id="100283" level="8">
    <if_sid>60154, 60160, 60170</if_sid>
    <description>T1098 Local group membership changed (re-attributed from T1484)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1098</id>
    </mitre>
  </rule>

  <!--
    ===================================================================================================
    T1547.001 Registry Run Keys / Startup Folder
    Custom rule IDs 100290-100294 | levels 8-12

    MEASURED BASELINE (2026-08-08, 5+5 windows, atomics 1/3/7/12, 27 attack / 31 benign):
    the SIXTH failure mode, and the first one caused by a severity setting rather than by missing or
    wrong detection logic.

        92300  level 0   targetObject matches ...CurrentVersion\Run       -> T1547.001   SILENT
          92301  level 12  details matches \.(lnk|vbs|vba)                -> T1547.001
          92302  level 6   image matches reg\.exe                         -> T1547.001
          92303  level 12  details matches (VNC|tvnserver\.exe)           -> T1547.001

    Level 0 in Wazuh means matched-but-not-emitted. So Wazuh owns a correctly-tagged T1547.001 rule
    whose only job is to gate three narrow children. A Run value holding a plain .exe path, or
    powershell.exe with an inline download cradle, written by anything other than reg.exe, matches
    92300 and produces NOTHING.

    THE DECISIVE MEASUREMENT is a contrast INSIDE the technique. Atomic 1 writes HKCU\...\Run via
    reg.exe and alerts at level 6. Atomic 3 writes HKLM\...\RunOnce via PowerShell Set-ItemProperty and
    produces zero T1547.001 alerts. Same persistence, same outcome at next logon, detected or invisible
    depending only on which binary performed the write. Verified positively: the registry values were
    confirmed present with reg query BEFORE cleanup, because a technique that predicts silence cannot
    use "no alerts" as evidence the run worked.

    AND THE ONE CORRECT RULE IS ANTI-DISCRIMINATIVE. 92302 fires 4 attack / 5 benign - more often on the
    administrator than the attacker, because registering an updater at logon via reg.exe is exactly what
    the benign mirror does. The sole correct detection in the default ruleset has a likelihood ratio
    pointing the wrong way.

    THREE INDEPENDENT GAPS in one technique:
      (a) SEVERITY   - 92300 is silent for the general case               -> 100290, 100291
      (b) PATH       - 92300 requires RUN immediately after CURRENTVERSION, so
                       ...CurrentVersion\Policies\Explorer\Run (atomic 12) never reaches it. The
                       alternation is unanchored, so RUNONCE and RUNONCEEX match by accident: the same
                       regex is simultaneously too loose and too tight.  -> 100292
      (c) DATA SOURCE- the Startup FOLDER variant is EID 11, not 13. 0860-sysmon_id_11.xml DOES see it:
                       92200 (L6) matches [c-z]:\\(Windows\\Temp|Users).+\.(bat|cmd|lnk|...) and 92201
                       (L9) adds image=powershell/cscript/wscript. But both map it to
                       T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer + T1059 - Persistence read as Command and Control
                       plus Execution.                                    -> 100293, 100294

    ⚠️ ORDERING IS BY DESCENDING LEVEL, NOT BY LOAD ORDER OR ID. MEASURED, not assumed - an earlier
    version of this comment asserted load order and was wrong. Three smoke-test observations, all
    consistent, none consistent with load order:
        100291 (L8)  displaced  92302 (L6)   on a reg.exe Run-key write
        100294 (L10) displaced  92201 (L9)   on a powershell-created Startup .lnk
        100290 (L12) displaced  100291 (L8)  on an interpreter payload
    Among sibling children of one parent, the highest level wins. Consequences:

      * 92302 IS DISPLACED. A reg.exe Run-key write now alerts as 100291 (L8), not 92302 (L6). The
        levels are NOT being lowered to prevent this. Level 5 would hand the reg.exe case back to the
        vendor, but choosing a severity to manipulate evaluation order rather than to express how
        serious the behaviour is would be bad detection engineering. Autostart persistence is a level 8.
        The displacement is reported instead: custom-phase T1547.001 counts must be split into alerts
        RELABELLED from 92302 and alerts that are NEWLY VISIBLE, the same treatment T1136.001 needed.

      * 100290 outranks 100291 by level, so the severity tier works regardless of file position. It is
        still written first for readability.

      * 100293 WAS REMOVED AS UNREACHABLE. It chained from 92201 (L9) with a Startup-folder condition,
        but 100294 (L10) is 92201's sibling under 92200 and always wins for exactly those paths - so
        92201 can never be the deepest match there and its child can never be reached. ID 100293 is
        RETIRED - see RULE_ID_REGISTER note 9b. Do not reuse it.

      * ⚠️ A REASONING ERROR, recorded because the method matters more than the conclusion. On seeing
        the smoke test produce 100294 rather than 92201, I concluded that 92201 had never been matching
        the Startup .lnk at all and that 92200 (L6) was the rule misattributing it to T1105 - and I
        edited that "correction" into this comment. It was wrong. The smoke test ran AFTER 100294 was
        deployed, so 92201 was already being outranked; the observation said nothing about how the
        BASELINE had behaved. The measured phases settle it: 92201 fires 5 attack / 5 benign in
        baseline and 0 / 0 in custom, while 100294 picks up exactly 5 / 5. 92201 WAS the misattributing
        rule, the original analysis was correct, and the correction was the error.
        GENERAL RULE: a post-deployment observation cannot be used to infer pre-deployment behaviour.
        The baseline is the only evidence of what the default ruleset did, which is the entire reason
        this project measures a baseline phase before writing anything.

    ⚠️ BACKSLASHES ARE DOUBLED in Wazuh field values, so a literal backslash is \\\\ in these regexes.
    This is the same trap that made the csc.exe exclusion in export_labelled_alerts.py silently match
    nothing. The vendor rules above use the same convention - copy it, do not guess.
    ===================================================================================================
  -->

  <rule id="100290" level="12">
    <if_sid>92300</if_sid>
    <field name="win.eventdata.details" type="pcre2">(?i)(powershell|pwsh|cmd\.exe|mshta|rundll32|regsvr32|wscript|cscript|certutil|bitsadmin|-enc\b|-encodedcommand|FromBase64|https?:|\\\\AppData\\\\|\\\\Temp\\\\|\\\\Users\\\\Public\\\\|\.(bat|cmd|ps1|vbs|vbe|js|jse|hta|scr)\b)</field>
    <description>T1547.001 Run key persistence with interpreter or staged payload: $(win.eventdata.details)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1547.001</id>
    </mitre>
  </rule>

  <!-- The catch-all that removes the silence. No conditions beyond the parent: if 92300 matched, a Run
       key was written, and that is worth an alert. Level 8 not 10 - installers and updaters register at
       logon constantly, so severity is proportionate to how ordinary the act is, and the measured
       both-classes behaviour of 92302 says plainly that this cannot be treated as a verdict. -->
  <rule id="100291" level="8">
    <if_sid>92300</if_sid>
    <description>T1547.001 Autostart Run key written by $(win.eventdata.image)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1547.001</id>
    </mitre>
  </rule>

  <rule id="100292" level="10">
    <if_group>sysmon_event_13</if_group>
    <field name="win.eventdata.targetObject" type="pcre2">(?i)CURRENTVERSION\\\\(POLICIES\\\\EXPLORER\\\\RUN|EXPLORER\\\\USER SHELL FOLDERS)</field>
    <description>T1547.001 Autostart via policy Run key or Shell Folder redirection: $(win.eventdata.targetObject)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1547.001</id>
    </mitre>
  </rule>

  <!-- 100293 RETIRED - unreachable. See the ordering note above and RULE_ID_REGISTER note 9b.
       Do not reuse this ID; the register keeps retired numbers reserved so that a future reader
       matching alert IDs against the register never finds a gap they have to guess about. -->

  <rule id="100294" level="10">
    <if_sid>92200</if_sid>
    <field name="win.eventdata.targetFilename" type="pcre2">(?i)\\\\Start Menu\\\\Programs\\\\Startup\\\\</field>
    <description>T1547.001 Startup folder persistence written by $(win.eventdata.image) (re-attributed from T1105)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1547.001</id>
    </mitre>
  </rule>

  <!--
    ===================================================================================================
    T1112 Modify Registry
    Custom rule IDs 100260-100264 | levels 8-12

    MEASURED BASELINE (2026-08-08, 5+5 windows, atomics 1/2/33/60, 55 attack / 41 benign):
    the SEVENTH failure mode. Three default sources claim T1112 and not one of them can see a registry
    modification when it happens:

      (a) Wazuh FIM (594/597/598/750/751/752, L5). Fires only for paths in the agent's
          <windows_registry> allow-list - 20 paths, EVERY ONE under HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE, not one HKCU
          entry - and only at the scheduled scan: <frequency>43200</frequency>, twelve hours, no
          realtime registry option configured. A detonation window is ~20s plus a 120s buffer, so the
          scan interval is about 360x the entire observation window. MEASURED: zero FIM alerts across
          all ten windows, INCLUDING atomic 2, which writes to HKLM\...\CurrentVersion\Run - a path that
          IS on the allow-list. Allow-list membership makes no observable difference at incident
          timescales. Latency is the binding constraint, not scope.
      (b) 92304-92306, which key on one key (Classes\Folder\shell\open\command) and carry T1112 only as
          a secondary tag beside T1548.002. UAC-bypass rules, not registry rules.
      (c) 91813/91814, which read PowerShell Script Block Logging (EID 4104). Verified disabled on this
          endpoint by query, not assumed. They cannot fire.

    LATENT COVERAGE: the rule exists, it is correctly aimed at registry modification, it does emit an
    alert - and it cannot arrive in time to matter. Distinct from T1547.001's suppressed severity, where
    the rule matched and emitted nothing at all.

    ⚠️ THE ONE RULE THAT DOES FIRE IS A BROKEN REGEX. 92041 (L10, "Value added to registry key has
    Base64-like pattern", mapped to T1027 + T1112) lives in 0800-sysmon_id_1.xml - it is a PROCESS
    CREATION rule that never looks at the registry. It matches reg.exe command lines with:

        (?i)add.+\/d\s+(")?(?:[A-Za-z\d+\/]{4})*(?:[A-Za-z\d+\/]{3}=|[A-Za-z\d+\/]{2}==)?

    Every group after /d is * or ?, so ZERO repetitions satisfy the pattern. It matches any
    `reg add ... /d <anything>` - verified, including `add x /d ` with nothing following. Consequences:
      * It fired 30 attack / 15 benign, exactly one per `reg add` command issued (6 per attack run,
        3 per benign run). It is a reg.exe execution counter wearing a base64 detection's description.
      * T1027 Obfuscated Files is asserted falsely in all 45 cases.
      * It is BLIND to PowerShell registry modification. Atomic 60 and the benign mirror both use
        Set-ItemProperty, which creates no reg.exe process, so they produce nothing.
    A defect of PRECISION, not coverage - and precision defects are the ones that generate alert
    fatigue, because they fire constantly at level 10 while carrying no information.

    DESIGN. Two groups of rules, doing two different jobs:

      100260/100261/100262 detect on Sysmon EID 13, which is WRITER-AGNOSTIC. This is the substantive
      fix: it closes the PowerShell blind spot that 92041 cannot see by construction, because EID 13
      records the registry write itself rather than the process that issued it.

      100263/100264 REPAIR 92041 rather than duplicating it, chaining via if_sid the way 100280 did for
      60109. 100263 restores the precision the vendor regex intended (a real base64 test, minimum 16
      characters) and keeps T1027. 100264 catches everything else and drops the false T1027 claim,
      leaving T1112 alone. Together they turn a rule that is wrong 100% of the time into one that is
      right about what it saw.

    ⚠️ LEVEL ORDERING (see the T1547.001 note above): siblings are evaluated by DESCENDING LEVEL.
    100260 (L12) must outrank 100261 (L8) and does. 100263 (L12) must outrank 100264 (L10) and does.
    Both custom children of 92041 outrank nothing else, but they DO displace 92041 itself, which is the
    point - counts must be reported as relabelled, not as new detection.

    ⚠️ 100260 vs 100261 IS A DELIBERATE EXPERIMENT, not redundancy. 100260 requires the value to be set
    to ZERO - the direction that disables a security control. 100261 fires on any write to the same
    keys. The benign mirror ENABLES module logging (value 1) where the atomic DISABLES it (value 0), on
    the same key, by the same writer. So the prediction is that 100261 fires in BOTH classes and 100260
    fires in ONE - and if that holds, it is a genuine discriminator rather than a mirror artefact,
    because disabling security logging is intrinsically suspicious in a way that enabling it is not.
    This is the first rule in the study designed to test WHEN rule logic can separate the classes rather
    than to demonstrate that it cannot.

    ⚠️ Sysmon writes DWORD details as "DWORD (0x00000000)", not "0". The zero test is
    DWORD\s*\(0x0+\) - `0x0+\)` cannot match 0x00000001 because a non-zero digit precedes the paren.

    ⚠️ KNOWN LIMITATION OF 100260, found in the dry-run and left in deliberately. "Disable" is not
    always zero. EnableModuleLogging=0 disables logging, but DisableAntiSpyware=1 also disables a
    control - the polarity depends on whether the value name is phrased positively or negatively.
    100260 tests for zero and therefore MISSES every Disable*=1 setting, including Defender tampering,
    which is among the most important cases in the whole family.
    Enumerating the polarity of every security value name is exactly the brittle, list-maintenance
    approach this project criticises in the FIM allow-list, so it is not attempted. 100261 catches those
    writes at L8 regardless of value, and the residual gap is recorded as a limitation rather than
    papered over with a longer regex. This is a concrete instance of the general finding: value-content
    rules need a semantic model of what each value MEANS, which is precisely the knowledge a rule engine
    does not have and a triage model has to supply.
    ===================================================================================================
  -->

  <rule id="100260" level="12">
    <if_group>sysmon_event_13</if_group>
    <field name="win.eventdata.targetObject" type="pcre2">(?i)\\\\(Policies\\\\Microsoft\\\\Windows\\\\(PowerShell|EventLog|System|WindowsUpdate)|Policies\\\\Microsoft\\\\Windows Defender|Microsoft\\\\Windows Defender|CurrentControlSet\\\\Services\\\\(WinDefend|EventLog|Sysmon))\\\\</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.details" type="pcre2">(?i)DWORD\s*\(0x0+\)</field>
    <description>T1112 Security control or logging policy DISABLED via registry: $(win.eventdata.targetObject)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1112</id>
      <id>T1562.001</id>
    </mitre>
  </rule>

  <rule id="100261" level="8">
    <if_group>sysmon_event_13</if_group>
    <field name="win.eventdata.targetObject" type="pcre2">(?i)\\\\(Policies\\\\Microsoft\\\\Windows\\\\(PowerShell|EventLog|System|WindowsUpdate)|Policies\\\\Microsoft\\\\Windows Defender|Microsoft\\\\Windows Defender|CurrentControlSet\\\\Services\\\\(WinDefend|EventLog|Sysmon))\\\\</field>
    <description>T1112 Security control or logging policy modified: $(win.eventdata.targetObject)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1112</id>
    </mitre>
  </rule>

  <!--
    ⚠️ PRECISION FIX 2026-08-09, found while measuring T1218.011 - the same way rule 100200's two
    defects were found, by watching an existing rule behave on the NEXT technique's traffic.

    The original condition was `\\Internet Settings\\(ZoneMap|Zones)\\`, which matched anywhere under
    ZoneMap. Measured over the whole dataset: 414 alerts, of which

        98  ZoneMap\ProxyBypass          <- Windows background housekeeping
        98  ZoneMap\IntranetName         <- "
        98  ZoneMap\UNCAsIntranet        <- "
        98  ZoneMap\AutoDetect           <- "
        11  ZoneMap\ProtocolDefaults\http    <- the actual technique
        11  ZoneMap\ProtocolDefaults\https   <- "

    95% noise. It swamped T1218.011 with 88 attack / 108 benign alerts - four times every other rule in
    that technique combined - and would have swamped every remaining technique too.

    THIS IS THE SAME DEFECT CLASS I DOCUMENTED IN 92041 THE PREVIOUS DAY, written while documenting it.
    That is worth stating plainly rather than quietly fixing: a broad path regex looks correct in review
    because every string it matches is genuinely "in the right area", and only measurement against
    unrelated traffic exposes it. It is not a vendor failing, it is a property of writing detections by
    pattern - which is the argument the whole project makes.

    ⚠️ IT WAS ALSO SELF-INFLICTED TWICE OVER: these events only exist because we widened the Sysmon
    config for T1112's third phase. Adding telemetry improved coverage AND created the noise, in the
    same change. That tradeoff is the alert-fatigue result demonstrated on our own ruleset.

    Narrowed to the subkeys where a write is security-relevant: ProtocolDefaults (protocol-to-zone
    mapping), Domains/EscDomains (adding a site to a trusted zone), and Zones (per-zone security
    settings such as enabling scripting). ZoneMap's scalar housekeeping values are excluded.

    ⚠️ ALREADY-COLLECTED ALERTS KEEP rule id 100262 - a rule change is not retroactive. T1112's recorded
    counts are therefore split at analysis time on target_object rather than by re-running the phase.
  -->
  <rule id="100262" level="8">
    <if_group>sysmon_event_13</if_group>
    <field name="win.eventdata.targetObject" type="pcre2">(?i)\\\\Internet Settings\\\\(ZoneMap\\\\(ProtocolDefaults|Domains|EscDomains)|Zones)\\\\</field>
    <description>T1112 Internet security zone configuration modified: $(win.eventdata.targetObject)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1112</id>
    </mitre>
  </rule>

  <!-- Restores the precision 92041's regex was meant to have. A base64 blob worth alerting on is at
       least 16 characters of the base64 alphabet, optionally padded - not "any run of zero or more
       four-character groups", which is what the vendor pattern reduces to. T1027 is retained HERE
       because here it is actually true. -->
  <rule id="100263" level="12">
    <if_sid>92041</if_sid>
    <field name="win.eventdata.commandLine" type="pcre2">(?i)\s\/d\s+"?[A-Za-z0-9+\/]{16,}={0,2}\b</field>
    <description>T1112 Registry value written with a genuine base64 payload via reg.exe</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1027</id>
      <id>T1112</id>
    </mitre>
  </rule>

  <!-- Everything else 92041 matched, which on measurement is everything: a plain reg add. Correctly
       T1112, and NOT T1027 - the vendor rule asserts obfuscation that is not present, and an alert that
       is wrong about why it fired is worse than no alert, because an analyst acts on the reason. -->
  <rule id="100264" level="10">
    <if_sid>92041</if_sid>
    <description>T1112 Registry value written via reg.exe (re-attributed from T1027 Obfuscated Files)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1112</id>
    </mitre>
  </rule>

  <!--
    ===================================================================================================
    T1218.011 System Binary Proxy Execution: Rundll32
    Custom rule IDs 100300-100302 | levels 6-12

    MEASURED BASELINE (2026-08-09, 5+5 windows, atomics 9/15/16, 32 attack / 24 benign):
    ZERO T1218.011 attribution from any rule. Only two default rules claim the technique and neither
    can see ordinary rundll32 abuse:

      92079 (L10) chains from 92078, which requires currentDirectory=E:\ AND cmd.exe in the command
            line - an ISO-mount scenario - and 92078 is itself mapped to T1204.002 User Execution.
            This endpoint has no E: drive, so the only general-looking rundll32 rule is STRUCTURALLY
            DEAD here: gated behind a condition that cannot occur.
      92081 (L15) needs the payload to be .html/.htm/.txt/.png/.jpg/.pdf with the ,#ordinal syntax.

    What fired instead: 92032 (T1087 + T1059.003), 92052 and 92004 (T1059.003), 92027 (T1059.001).
    Defense Evasion read as Execution and Account Discovery - the same wrong-tactic pattern measured on
    T1082, T1016 and T1112.

    DESIGN - three tiers, because rundll32 is not intrinsically suspicious and pretending otherwise
    would produce a rule with a worse false-positive rate than the noise it replaces:

      100302 (L12) script protocol handlers - vbscript:, javascript:, RunHTMLApplication. No legitimate
             administrative use whatsoever; this is parser abuse.
      100300 (L10) the known LOLBAS proxy-execution exports. Real technique, but every one of these has
             a legitimate caller, so this is a lead rather than a verdict.
      100301 (L6)  any rundll32 loading a DLL,Export at all. Rundll32 runs constantly on a live desktop,
             so the level is deliberately low - proportionate to how ordinary the act is.

    ⚠️ PREDICTION: 100300 WILL FIRE IN BOTH CLASSES. The benign mirror uses pcwutl.dll,LaunchApplication
    and url.dll,FileProtocolHandler - the same two exports as atomics 9 and 15 - because those ARE what
    the Program Compatibility Wizard and the Windows file-handler do. A rule keyed on the export list
    therefore cannot separate the classes, and that is the honest result for this technique rather than
    a defect to engineer around.

    ⚠️ 100302 CANNOT BE EXERCISED BY THIS TEST SET. Atomic 2, the only vbscript: test, was excluded
    because it never terminates (rundll32 persists as an HTA host until ART's 120s timeout, inflating
    its window to 193s). The rule is deployed and SMOKE-TESTED MANUALLY OUTSIDE ANY DETONATION WINDOW,
    so its correctness is demonstrated without contaminating the measurement. Expect 0/0 in both phases,
    and record that as "not exercised", NOT as "did not fire" - the two mean opposite things and
    conflating them is how a rule that does not work gets recorded as a rule that found nothing.

    ⚠️ DISPLACEMENT EXPECTED. Sibling rules are evaluated by descending level, so 100301 (L6) outranks
    92032 (L3) on any rundll32 process that 92032 also matched. Custom-phase counts must be split into
    relabelled and newly-detected, as for every technique since T1136.001.

    ⚠️ Harness artefact already visible in the baseline: 92027 is attack-only (5/0) because ART's
    powershell executor spawns a child powershell.exe for atomic 16, while the mirror routes everything
    through cmd.exe. Same artefact recorded on T1082. Exclude from separability claims.
    ===================================================================================================
  -->

  <rule id="100302" level="12">
    <if_group>sysmon_eid1_detections</if_group>
    <field name="win.eventdata.originalFileName" type="pcre2">(?i)^RUNDLL32\.EXE$</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.commandLine" type="pcre2">(?i)(vbscript:|javascript:|RunHTMLApplication)</field>
    <description>T1218.011 Rundll32 proxy execution via script protocol handler</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1218.011</id>
    </mitre>
  </rule>

  <rule id="100300" level="10">
    <if_group>sysmon_eid1_detections</if_group>
    <field name="win.eventdata.originalFileName" type="pcre2">(?i)^RUNDLL32\.EXE$</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.commandLine" type="pcre2">(?i)(pcwutl\.dll,\s*LaunchApplication|url\.dll,\s*(FileProtocolHandler|OpenURL)|zipfldr\.dll,\s*RouteTheCall|shell32\.dll,\s*(Control_RunDLL|ShellExec_RunDLL)|(ie)?advpack\.dll,\s*(LaunchINFSection|RegisterOCX)|(syssetup|setupapi)\.dll,\s*(InstallHinfSection|SetupInfObjectInstallAction)|desk\.cpl,\s*InstallScreenSaver|dfshim\.dll,\s*ShOpenVerbApplication|comsvcs\.dll,\s*MiniDump)</field>
    <description>T1218.011 Rundll32 proxy execution via known LOLBAS export: $(win.eventdata.commandLine)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1218.011</id>
    </mitre>
  </rule>

  <rule id="100301" level="6">
    <if_group>sysmon_eid1_detections</if_group>
    <field name="win.eventdata.originalFileName" type="pcre2">(?i)^RUNDLL32\.EXE$</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.commandLine" type="pcre2">(?i)\.(dll|cpl|ocx|inf)\s*,\s*\S</field>
    <description>T1218.011 Rundll32 loaded a DLL export: $(win.eventdata.commandLine)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1218.011</id>
    </mitre>
  </rule>

</group>
